11:13:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Excellent question — and you're right that those *are* fundamentally different from regular planes. This actually reveals an important distinction in how thrust can be used. ## The Key Difference: Thrust Direction | Aircraft type | Thrust direction | What balances gravity | |---|---|---| | **Regular jet** | Horizontal (forward) | Wings' lift | | **Harrier (hovering)** | Straight down | Engine thrust directly | | **Rocket** | Straight down (at launch) | Engine thrust directly | In a Harrier doing vertical takeoff, the pilot literally rotates the engine nozzles to point straight down. In that mode, **you're right** — thrust is directly countering gravity, with no wings involved at all. Same with a rocket: thrust from the engine directly opposes weight. ## Why Regular Planes Don't Work This Way A regular jet engine's thrust points backward, roughly parallel to the ground. That force has **no vertical component** to speak of — it can't push against gravity directly, no matter how powerful it is. It can only accelerate the plane forward, which then generates lift via the wings. Try to imagine strapping a regular 737 engine facing backward and trying to "hover" — it wouldn't work, because none of the thrust points downward. It doesn't matter how much power you add; you can't out-muscle gravity if your force vector points the wrong way. ## Why Harriers Can Do This But Airliners Can't (Practically) 1. **Thrust-to-weight ratio**: To hover, an aircraft's engines must produce thrust *greater than or equal to* the aircraft's entire weight — straight up. Harrier engines are built for this ratio; airliner engines are not (they're optimized for efficient forward cruising, not brute vertical lift). 2. **Fuel consumption**: Hovering via pure thrust is incredibly fuel-hungry, since you're fighting gravity directly with no help from aerodynamic lift. This is why Harriers and rockets can only sustain this for short periods (rockets discard huge amounts of fuel mass fast; Harriers hover only briefly). 3. **Efficiency of wings**: Lift is far more fuel-efficient than raw thrust for sustained flight, because wings essentially get "help" from the surrounding air for free. That's why once a Harrier transitions to forward flight, it redirects its nozzles backward and starts relying on its wings, just like a regular plane. ## So You've Identified Two Real Categories - **Thrust-supported flight** (rockets, hovering Harriers, hovering drones): engines directly oppose gravity. - **Lift-supported flight** (regular planes, gliders, hovering birds... sort of): wings oppose gravity; engines just maintain speed. Both are legitimate ways to fly — they're just different physics regimes depending on which direction the thrust points and whether wings are involved at all.
