11:14:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is the crux of it, and there's a good reason to keep the distinction — because "engines keep planes up" makes a **wrong prediction** in a very real scenario: the **stall**. ## The Stall: Where the Simple Version Breaks Imagine a plane flying with **full engine power** — engines roaring, absolutely maxed out — but the pilot pitches the nose up too steeply. What happens? **The plane falls.** Not glides — actually drops, sometimes violently, despite the engines working perfectly and at full strength. This is called a **stall**, and it happens because the wings lose their ability to generate lift when the angle of attack (the angle between the wing and oncoming air) gets too steep, regardless of engine power. Air stops flowing smoothly over the wing and separates into turbulence. This is a major cause of real aviation accidents — pilots have crashed perfectly good aircraft, engines running fine the whole time, because the *wings* stopped producing lift. ## Why This Matters for Your Question If "engines keep the plane up" were literally, mechanistically true, then **more engine power should always mean more altitude or at least prevent falling**. But a stall proves that's false — you can have maximum thrust and still fall out of the sky, because thrust and lift are genuinely separate things governed by separate variables (angle of attack, airspeed direction relative to the wing, etc.), not just "power on/off." ## So Where Does That Leave Us? - **As a practical/causal shorthand** ("you need engines or the plane won't fly") — you're basically right, and nobody would fault you for saying it that way in casual conversation. - **As a mechanistic explanation** ("the engines are the thing physically holding the plane up") — this is where it breaks down, because it can't explain stalls, gliders, or why thrust direction matters (per the Harrier discussion). The wing talk isn't overcomplicating things for its own sake — it's the only way to explain cases where the simple version gives the wrong answer. That's usually the real test of whether a simplification is "fine" or "misleading": does it make correct predictions in edge cases? Here, it doesn't, which is why aerospace engineers, pilots, and physics textbooks all maintain the distinction, even though in 95% of everyday flight the two forces move together and the difference barely seems to matter.
